  "summary": "The pale mane and tail of a chestnut horse - from a few gold strands, or light and dark hairs mixed, to a near-white fall of hair - held here as a dosage of two made-up alleles. No gene, locus, variant or DNA test for flaxen has been published, so no position can be given; a candidate region on chromosome 14 is claimed only in sources that are not peer-reviewed. It is heritable: in Morgans especially, flaxen chestnuts bred together often give flaxen foals, and it can pass unseen through bay or black relatives. Breeders have long taken it for a recessive, but that rests on pedigrees, not on a published study, and the wide range of intensity fits no simple single-locus model. It touches only the mane and tail (perhaps feathering), only on a chestnut, and nothing else. Look-alikes have known genes and should be ruled out first: palomino (one cream), champagne on chestnut, mushroom, and silver, which lightens the mane and tail of a bay or black, not a chestnut.",
